TITLE: High-Dose Nicotine Replacement Therapy for Quitting Cigarettes and E-cigarettes
SUMMARY: This randomized clinical trial evaluates the effectiveness of augmented-dose combination nicotine replacement therapy (NRT) versus standard-dose NRT and no-NRT control for cessation of dual combustible cigarette (CC) and electronic cigarette (EC) use. Adults who currently engage in dual use and are motivated to quit both products will be assigned to one of three 12-week interventions: (1) no pharmacotherapy (behavioral support only), (2) standard-dose NRT (21 mg nicotine patch + 4 mg lozenges, 5-20/day), or (3) augmented-dose NRT (21 mg + 14 mg patches + 4 mg lozenges, 5-30/day). All groups receive evidence-based behavioral support through the EX Program. Primary outcomes include biochemically confirmed dual abstinence and CC-only abstinence at 12 and 24 weeks. A subset of participants will provide blood and saliva samples to assess DNA damage biomarkers associated with tobacco use and cessation.
INCLUSION_CRITERIA:
age 18+;
report nicotine-containing e-cigarette use 5+ times per day, \>5 days per week, for \> 6 months;
smoking \>1 cigarette per day, \>5 days per week, for \>6 months;
interest in quitting cigarettes and e-cigarettes within the next month (\>6 on 10-point scale);
willing to try nicotine replacement therapy;
access to text/internet/email.

